# Response headers for every route. This file is the single source of truth
# for the portal's security headers: build.js copies it into dist/, and both
# Cloudflare Pages and Netlify read it from the publish directory, so the same
# rules apply on either host during and after the migration off Netlify.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# index.html already carries a <meta http-equiv="Content-Security-Policy">.
# A meta CSP is honoured for most directives, but the spec requires user agents
# to IGNORE frame-ancestors when it arrives that way — it takes effect only as
# an HTTP response header. The portal was therefore declaring clickjacking
# protection it did not actually have. Serving the policy as a real header is
# what closes that, and lets the transport/browser headers below be set too.
#
# KEEP THE HEADER POLICY AND THE META POLICY IN SYNC. A browser receiving both
# enforces the INTERSECTION of them, so tightening a directive in one place and
# not the other breaks the app in a way that is painful to trace.
# tests/securityHeaders.test.js fails the suite if they disagree on any shared
# directive. Rationale for each header lives in docs/SECURITY.md §7.
#
# connect-src carries the Supabase REST + realtime origins and the Open-Meteo
# forecast endpoint behind the dashboard weather card; everything else is
# same-origin because React, react-dom and supabase-js are all vendored.

/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: https:; connect-src 'self' https://*.supabase.co wss://*.supabase.co https://api.open-meteo.com; frame-ancestors 'self'; form-action 'self'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self';
  X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=()
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains
